 "language": "en",
 "text": "The first Europeans to live in Australia arrived 159 years before the First Fleet, and they were left there as punishment. 1629, the Dutch ship Batavia smashes into a reef off Western Australia. While the commander sails for help, a merchant named Jeronimus Cornelisz takes over the survivors and starts killing them. More than a hundred people die. But a soldier named Wiebbe Hayes and his men hold out on another island. They build stone walls to defend themselves, now thought to be the oldest European structures in Australia. When rescue finally arrives, the mutineers are caught. Cornelisz is executed. But two young mutineers get a different sentence. They're dumped on the mainland. Their names were Wouter Loos and Jan Pelgrom. They were never seen again. Australia's first European residents, and nobody knows what happened to them.",
